Understanding Production Kubernetes: A Real-World Scenario
Let me paint a picture of how Kubernetes actually works in a real company, from Day 1 to managing thousands of requests.

The Problem Kubernetes Solves
Imagine you work at a startup that built an e-commerce app. Initially, you had one server running your website. As traffic grew, you needed multiple servers. Then came the nightmare: managing which server runs what, auto-scaling when traffic spikes, handling server failures, updating code without downtime, and managing storage across servers.

This is where Kubernetes came in. It's like hiring a smart operations team that handles all this automatically.

Real Production Scenario: An E-Commerce App
Let me walk you through what happens in production at a company running Kubernetes:

The Application Stack
Your company has:

Frontend (React website) - 3 copies running
Backend API (Node.js) - 5 copies running
Database (PostgreSQL) - 1 main + 1 backup
Cache (Redis) - 1 instance
Job Queue (for sending emails) - 2 workers
Without Kubernetes: You'd manually:

SSH into each server and start/stop services
Monitor CPU usage on each server
Update code and restart services
Handle failures by manually restarting services
Manually add new servers when traffic spikes
With Kubernetes: Everything is automated!

How a Production Kubernetes Cluster Works
