    targetPort: 3000
Copy
What's really happening here in production?

Resource Requests & Limits: This is CRITICAL! Without this, one buggy pod can consume all server memory and crash other applications.

Pod 1 needs 500MB (request) but can use up to 800MB (limit)
Pod 2 needs 500MB (request) but can use up to 800MB (limit)
Pod 3 needs 500MB (request) but can use up to 800MB (limit)
Pod 4 needs 500MB (request) but can use up to 800MB (limit)
Pod 5 needs 500MB (request) but can use up to 800MB (limit)

Total Server RAM = 8GB
Reserved for system = 1GB
Available = 7GB

Kubernetes says: "I can safely fit all 5 pods (5 × 500MB = 2.5GB needed)"
Health Checks: Your app might be running but returning errors. Health checks tell Kubernetes the real status:

Liveness Probe: "Is your app still alive?" 
- Checks every 10 seconds
- If fails 3 times, Kubernetes kills the pod and creates a new one

Readiness Probe: "Are you ready to handle traffic?"
- Checks every 5 seconds
- If fails, Kubernetes stops sending traffic here but doesn't kill it
- Good for startup time: container might be starting databases
Rolling Update Strategy: When you push new code (v2.5.0), Kubernetes doesn't stop all servers at once:

Time 0:  [v2.4.0] [v2.4.0] [v2.4.0] [v2.4.0] [v2.4.0]  ← All running
Time 1:  [v2.5.0] [v2.4.0] [v2.4.0] [v2.4.0] [v2.4.0]  ← Deploy 1 new
Time 2:  [v2.5.0] [v2.5.0] [v2.4.0] [v2.4.0] [v2.4.0]  ← Deploy 2 new
Time 3:  [v2.5.0] [v2.5.0] [v2.5.0] [v2.4.0] [v2.4.0]  ← Keep going...
Time 4:  [v2.5.0] [v2.5.0] [v2.5.0] [v2.5.0] [v2.4.0]  ← Almost done
Time 5:  [v2.5.0] [v2.5.0] [v2.5.0] [v2.5.0] [v2.5.0]  ← Complete!

Zero downtime! Customers never notice!
Real Daily Operations in Production
Morning: Traffic Spike
Your e-commerce site has a flash sale at 9 AM. Traffic suddenly 10x.

WITHOUT Kubernetes:
- 9:02 AM: Alert! Website is slow!
- 9:05 AM: Engineer manually requests 5 new servers from cloud provider
- 9:15 AM: Servers arrive
- 9:20 AM: Engineer manually configures each server
- 9:25 AM: Deploy code to new servers
- 9:30 AM: Finally handling all traffic
Result: 30 minutes of slow website = lost customers

WITH Kubernetes:
- 9:01 AM: CPU usage goes to 80%
- 9:01:30 AM: Autoscaler detects high CPU
- 9:02 AM: Kubernetes automatically creates 3 more pods
- 9:02:30 AM: New servers received and pods scheduled
- 9:03 AM: All traffic handled, customers happy!
Here's what you'd configure:

CopyapiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: backend-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: backend-api
  minReplicas: 5      # Always keep at least 5
  maxReplicas: 50     # Never go above 50
  metrics:
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: cpu
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 70
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: memory
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 80
  behavior:
    scaleDown:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300  # Wait 5 min before scaling down
    scaleUp:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 0    # Scale up immediately
Translation: "If my 5 pods are at 70% CPU, add more pods. If at 80% memory, add more. When traffic dies down, gradually remove pods (but wait 5 minutes to make sure it's not a temporary dip)."

Real Production Problems & How Kubernetes Handles Them
Problem 1: Server Hardware Fails
Real Scenario:

2 AM, Server in data center catches fire
Your database pod is running there
Your website goes down
Without Kubernetes: Disaster recovery team scrambles, website down for hours

With Kubernetes:

You configured: persistentVolumeClaim: postgres-volume pointing to network storage
Your pod is gone, but data is safe on shared storage
Kubernetes automatically restarts the database pod on another server
Website back up in 2 minutes
Morning team doesn't even know it happened
Problem 2: One Pod Goes Crazy
Real Scenario:

A bug causes one backend pod to use 2GB of RAM instead of 500MB
It tries to use more, hits the limit of 800MB
Kubernetes immediately kills it
Starts a new one
Other 4 pods keep serving customers
Logs show the crash, engineers debug it next morning
Pod Status: CrashLoopBackOff
Last Log: "OutOfMemory: Cannot allocate memory"
→ Engineer finds memory leak
→ Fixes bug
→ Pushes v2.5.1
→ Kubernetes automatically updates pods
→ Problem solved
Problem 3: Database Backup Required
Without Kubernetes:

You manually SSH into DB server
Run backup command
Wait for it to finish
Backup to external storage
Manual process = can forget to do it
With Kubernetes:

CopyapiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: db-backup
spec:
  schedule: "0 2 * * *"  # Every day at 2 AM
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      template:
        spec:
          containers:
          - name: backup
            image: postgres:15
            command: ["pg_dump", "-h", "postgres-service", "-U", "postgres", "-d", "mydb", "-f", "/backup/db-$(date +%Y%m%d).sql"]
