kubectl delete pod my-pod
pod "my-pod" deleted

# Check pods
kubectl get pods
No resources found
It's gone forever! Nothing restarts it because there's no controller (like Deployment) managing it.

When to Use kubectl run:
Copy# Quick testing - "Does this image work?"
kubectl run test --image=nginx

# Debugging - "Let me explore inside a container"
kubectl run -it debug-shell --image=busybox -- sh

# One-off jobs - "Run this script once"
kubectl run my-script --image=python:3.9 -- python /script.py
Real Example: Debugging a Database
Copy# You want to test database connectivity
kubectl run -it db-test --image=postgres:15 -- psql -h postgres-service -U admin -d mydb -c "SELECT * FROM users LIMIT 5;"

# This runs a container, connects to database, shows results, and disappears
# No mess, no leftover resources
Command 2: kubectl create deployment my-app --image=nginx
What Actually Happens:
Copykubectl create deployment my-app --image=nginx
This creates a Deployment, which automatically creates a ReplicaSet, which manages a Pod.

Kubernetes Cluster
│
└── Deployment: my-app
    └── ReplicaSet: my-app-5d4m8s
        └── Pod: my-app-5d4m8s-xxxxx
            └── Container: nginx
                └── Image: nginx
Real Output:
