Understanding the Architecture Layer by Layer
What kubectl run Creates:
Copy# This is what kubectl run actually creates
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: my-pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: my-pod
    image: nginx
Just a Pod. That's it. No controller managing it.

What kubectl create deployment Creates:
Copy# Step 1: The Deployment (what you created)
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: my-app
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: my-app
        image: nginx

---
# Step 2: The ReplicaSet (Deployment creates this)
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: ReplicaSet
metadata:
  name: my-app-5d4m8s
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-app
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: my-app
        image: nginx

---
# Step 3: The Pod (ReplicaSet creates this)
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: my-app-5d4m8s-xxxxx
  labels:
    app: my-app
spec:
  containers:
  - name: my-app
    image: nginx
Copy
Three nested controllers! Each layer adds capabilities:

Pod: Basic container running
ReplicaSet: "Keep N copies running" (self-healing)
Deployment: "Manage updates, rollbacks, scaling"
Practical Examples: When to Use What
Example 1: Testing if an Image Works
Copy# You just created a Docker image, want to test it quickly
kubectl run test-nginx --image=myregistry/nginx:latest

# If it works: delete it and move on
kubectl delete pod test-nginx

# ✅ Perfect use case for kubectl run
# Why? One-off test, no need for persistent running
Example 2: Running a Utility Pod for Debugging
Copy# You need to debug database connectivity
# Create a temporary container with database tools
kubectl run -it db-debug \
  --image=postgres:15 \
  -- psql -h postgres-service -U admin -d mydb

# Type SQL commands, then exit
# Pod is deleted

# ✅ Perfect use case for kubectl run
# Why? Temporary debugging tool, ephemeral
Example 3: Running a Web Server
Copy# You need to deploy a web app that should always be running
# Even if pod crashes, it should restart
kubectl create deployment nginx-web --image=nginx

# If you want 3 copies for redundancy:
kubectl scale deployment nginx-web --replicas=3

# If you want to update the image:
kubectl set image deployment/nginx-web nginx=nginx:1.25

# If something goes wrong, rollback:
kubectl rollout undo deployment/nginx-web

# ✅ Perfect use case for kubectl create deployment
# Why? Production workload, needs recovery, scaling, updates
Example 4: Database Pod
Copy# DON'T use: kubectl run postgres-db --image=postgres:15
# This pod dies → data lost forever!

# DO use: kubectl create statefulset postgres --image=postgres:15
# StatefulSet: like Deployment but for stateful apps
# - Persistent storage
- Stable network identity
- Automatic recovery

# ✅ Perfect use case for kubectl create statefulset
# Why? Data must survive pod restart
